==Performance analysis profiler
"Coz: Causal Profiling"
https://github.com/plasma-umass/coz
* measures optimization potential: predicts what the impact of optimizing code
  will have on overall throughput or latency

"Dthreads: efficient deterministic multithreading"
https://github.com/emeryberger/dthreads
* usage of processes with ipc instead of threads to enforce determinism
* pretty much erlang with beam etc

https://www.brendangregg.com/blog/2024-03-17/the-return-of-the-frame-pointers.html
on stack walking
* LBR (Last Branch Record): Intel's hardware feature that was limited to 16 or 32 frames
* BTS (Branch Trace Store): Another Intel thing
* AET (Archetectural Event Trace): Another Intel thing. It's a JTAG-based tracer
  that can trace low-level CPU, BIOS, and device events, and apparently can be
  used for stack traces as well.
* DWARF: high overhead since designed for non-realtime use for debuggers
* eBPF stack walking (SystemTap): brittle due to shipping language pieces
* ORC (oops rewind capability): Can't ORCs invade user space as well?
* SFrames (Stack Frames): revision V2 significant VM size overhead and the lack
  of clear advantages over established alternatives
* Shadow Stacks (Intel and AMD feature): other blog posts sound like slowdown
  is currently too high
* There are much more specialized solutions
Hence
* keep frame pointers and pay <1% perf penalty to have proper stack traces for
  performance analysis (even though there is inaccuracy that needs mitigation)
* SFrames may become default, once the problems are hashed out (which are currently not)
* Shadow Stacks may become default, once the problems are hashed out (which are currently not)

